+///
+/// Byte offsets only, never `str` slicing at the prefix/suffix boundary itself: every slice this
+/// function actually takes is bounded by a `paragraph_ranges` result, which is always aligned to a
+/// `'\n'` (so always a valid UTF-8 boundary) regardless of where the raw byte diff landed.
+///
+/// Also returns the range of *new* paragraph indices that were actually reshaped (`lead..len -
+/// tail`): every index outside it is the exact same [`TextParagraph`] (same `parley::Layout`
+/// instance, only its `range` possibly shifted) as before this call, which is what lets
